 ## Subtopics
 
 ### [BFS Light Propagation](2164-bfs-light-propagation.md)
-BFS Light Propagation is a voxel-based lighting algorithm that propagates light levels from emitters across a padded voxel chunk using a Breadth-First Search (BFS). It solves the problem of efficiently illuminating non-opaque blocks in a voxel grid while attenuating light intensity with distance. It differs from its parent, [Slice Filling with BFS Propagation](1909-slice-filling-with-bfs-propagation.md), by operating on the full 3D padded chunk rather than just the slice plane, and by explicitly modeling light attenuation per step.
+BFS Light Propagation is a voxel lighting algorithm that computes indirect illumination by propagating light intensity from source voxels to adjacent non-opaque neighbors using a Breadth-First Search (BFS) queue. It solves the problem of efficiently filling interior voxel volumes with accurate light levels while respecting opaque block boundaries, serving as the core lighting pass for chunk-based voxel renderers. It is distinguished from its sibling [Slice Filling with BFS Propagation](1909-slice-filling-with-bfs-propagation.md) by operating on the full 3D padded chunk volume rather than being restricted to 2D slice planes, allowing light to propagate vertically and horizontally across the entire chunk.
+
+### [Flood Fill for Minkowski Sum Boundary Identification](14571-flood-fill-for-minkowski-sum-boundary-identification.md)
+This method identifies the outer boundary of a Minkowski sum by propagating a "filled" state from the exterior into the interior volume, stopping when blocked by the object's boundary. It builds on [Slice Filling with BFS Propagation](1909-slice-filling-with-bfs-propagation.md) by using a global iterative flood fill rather than slice-local propagation to determine which voxels are outside the Minkowski sum. Unlike Lien's method, which handles internal holes and generates exact boundary representations, this approach is approximate but significantly faster.
 
